{"text": "STATE ATTORNEY'S OFFICE\n\u2018IWELFTH JUDICIAL CIRCUIT OF FLORIDA\n\nINTERDEPARTMENTAL MEMORANDUM\n\nTo: SARASOTA SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nATTENTION DEP. EGAN, #2257; RECORDS/CID\nFROM: ANDREA MCHUGH\nDEFENDANT: MELANIE A TURNER\n\nCHARGE 'EMPTED SEXUAL BATTERY (FORCE NOT LIKELY TO CAUSE BODILY HARM\n= VICTIM 12 OR OLDER); FALSE IMPRISONMENT\nARREST DATE: 03/20/2014\nAGENCY #: 14-014505\nSAO CASE #:  14CF009641AS\n\nis office has made the following decision concerning the charges\nagainst the above-named defendant:\n\nNo Action will be taken on the charge(s) listed above.\nCOMMENT SECTION:\n\nThe defendant attended a party at the alleged victim\u2019s residence from which she is charged as a\nprincipal to sexual battery and false imprisonment. The defendant was an off-duty police officer with\nthe North Port Police Department. North Port Police transferred the investigation to the Sarasota\nSheriffs Office. The Sheriff's Office conducted a thorough investigation of the case, interviewing most\nwitnesses who attended the party, and conducting three interviews of the victim and other additional\npotential witnesses. Additionally, the Sheriff's Office conducted forensic examinations of the defendant\nand on-duty officer's cell phone, interviewed the co-defendant and the defendant twice.\n\u2018The undersigned conducted interviews of ten of the witnesses, consulted with the investigating\nagency, reviewed all of the witness statements given to law enforcement, reviewed the digital evidence\nobtained, and examined the applicable statutes and case law in order to determine what facts would be\npresented at trial.\nFacts:\n\nThe Prior Relationship between the Victim and Defendant:\n\nThe relationship and exchanges between the victim and defendant prior to the act in question\nwould be relevant and admissible at trial because the issue in this case is whether or not the victim\nconsented to the activity. Minus v, State, 901 So.2d 344 (Fla. 4th DCA 2005). Therefore, the following\nevidence would be admissible for the jury's consideration in deciding if the defendant reasonably\nbelieved the victim was consenting to the sexual activity. The victim and defendant had been close\nfriends in the months preceding the party. During their friendship, they had intimate discussions\nregarding their sex lives, including the fact that the victim engaged in group sexual activities for a\nnumber of years with her ex-husband, friend of the victim witnessed the victim and defendant kiss on\n4 prior occasion. The victim and defendant exchanged group text messages including images of\ndisrobed males, and in one communication, the victim wrote, \u201cwhip me beat me show me who's boss.\u201d\nIn the months leading up to the night in question, the victim and defendant hosted and attended parties\nat each other\u2019s homes that involved consuming alcohol and activities with sexual connotations. One",
